# web

Web access for `luxe chat`: bounded HTTP fetch, HTML→markdown extraction,
optional headless-browser rendering, provider-backed search, and grounded
answers.

**Search and answers are DIFFERENT features** (2026-08-03): `web_search`
returns ranked links the model then reads via `web_fetch`; `web_answer`
(answers.py — Brave Answers, an OpenAI-compatible chat-completions endpoint
at /res/v1/chat/completions) returns ONE server-side-synthesized grounded
answer. They are separate Brave subscriptions with separate billing, keyed
separately: `BRAVE_API_KEY` (search) vs `BRAVE_ANSWERS_API_KEY` (answers).
Each tool is withheld independently when its own key does not resolve. Do
not fold one into the other or share a key between them.

Added 2026-07-31. Every surface here is CHAT-ONLY and gated behind `/web`
(default OFF). The package exists because a coding agent that cannot read
documentation is guessing; it is gated because luxe is the offline fallback
kit and network egress from a tool is a capability you opt into.

**Single stack (2026-08-03).** This package is the ONLY web/browser surface in
luxe. It absorbed `src/luxe/browser.py` (`browse_navigate`/`browse_read` over
headless-Chrome CDP, `[browser]` extra = pychrome + trafilatura), which had
landed independently two days earlier — the two were built in parallel by
different sessions and neither knew about the other. What was kept from it:
the optional host allowlist (now `LUXE_WEB_ALLOWLIST`, layered on the IP guard
rather than replacing it) and the system-Chrome fallback (so the Chromium
download is optional). What was dropped: the second tool pair, the second
browser dependency, the second extra, and always-on gating. Do not
reintroduce any of them.

## Must
- Stay OUT of `TOOL_FNS`/`MUTATION_FNS`. These tools reach the live internet,
  so a benchmark or maintain run that could call them would stop being
  reproducible. They reach a turn ONLY through `run_single`'s extra-tool seam,
  from `prepare_turn`, and only when `session.web_enabled` is set
- Apply `fetch._assert_public` to EVERY URL before any request, and again to
  every redirect hop and post-render final URL. luxe sits on a tailnet beside
  privileged mage-hands relays, an oMLX endpoint on localhost, and a NAS: an
  ungated fetch tool is an SSRF weapon pointed at the operator's own fleet
- Resolve the hostname to check it. A textual blocklist is theatre —
  `localtest.me` and countless other public names resolve to 127.0.0.1
- Bound every request twice: a per-read timeout AND a total-wall deadline.
  httpx timeouts are per-read, so a trickling response outlives any of them
  (the same trap `backend.py` documents for keepalives)
- Cap response bytes during streaming, before decode — never materialize an
  unbounded body into a chat turn
- Return `(str, str | None)` from every tool fn and never raise into the agent
  loop (tools.sdd)
- Report emptiness honestly: a page that extracted to almost nothing must say
  so and name the `render=true` retry, not return a blank-looking success
- Keep the interactive page session (`page.py`, tool `web_page`, 2026-08-05)
  on ONE dedicated daemon thread that owns the sync-Playwright driver: tool
  calls post ops and wait with a deadline (TUI turns run on varying worker
  threads and sync Playwright is thread-bound). One page per process; popups
  are closed at birth; a caller-deadline miss or an egress violation after
  ANY action HARD-CLOSES the session (a click can navigate anywhere — the
  guard re-runs on every hop, and non-public space is a stop, not a detour).
  `/web` off and interpreter exit close it; a cancelled turn merely abandons
  its wait and the session survives. Withhold `web_page` when
  `browser.availability()` says no. Form submissions that log in, purchase,
  post, or message ride the tool description's explicit-ask rule
- Withhold `web_search` / `web_answer` entirely when their respective key
  does not resolve. A tool that can only answer "no API key" is worse than
  no tool
- Resolve provider keys through `luxe.secrets` by env-var NAME only (env →
  ~/.luxe/secrets.env → Keychain). A key VALUE never appears in YAML or in
  this repo — same rule as the oMLX backends and the relay tokens
- Treat `LUXE_WEB_ALLOWLIST` as an ADDITIONAL layer, never a replacement for
  the IP-class guard. Unset = no host restriction; set = deny-by-default for
  hosts. A hostname allowlist cannot see where a name resolves, so on its own
  it does not close the tailnet/CGNAT hole `_is_public_ip` exists for

## Must not
- Make `playwright` a base or `[chat]` dependency. It is `[web]`, plus a
  separate ~150-400 MB `playwright install chromium`; the fallback kit has to
  install and run on every fleet host without it
- Raise `ImportError` at the model when Chromium is absent. `browser.availability()`
  is the single answer to "can we render?", and it names the exact fix
- Let the model lift the egress guard. `LUXE_WEB_ALLOW_PRIVATE=1` is an
  environment decision by the operator, never a tool argument
- Add a networked line to `/doctor`. Its offline-purity contract allows
  exactly one (`update`); the web check reads local keys and probes a local
  binary path only (chat.sdd)
- Tie `/web` to `/write`. Reading a page mutates nothing on disk; conflating
  the two would misprice both gates

## Owns
- src/luxe/web/**
- The `/web` command, `--web` startup flag, and `session.web_enabled`

## Forbids
- Inline prompt strings (agents.sdd: the registry is the single source). Tool
  DESCRIPTIONS live here because they are tool schema, not prompts
- Any write to the filesystem. This package reads the web and returns text;
  saving a page is `write_file`'s job, behind `/write`
